---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, breadcrumb } from '../data/site.ts';
import { MECHANISMS } from '../data/science.ts';
const title = 'The Fasolati Science Spine | OMVs, evidence, and the human bridge';
const description = 'Explore the OMV-to-systemic-effect map: what vesicle experiments show, what human evidence supports, and what remains to be validated for personal use.';
const schema = [{
  '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': `${SITE}/science/`, url: `${SITE}/science/`, name: title,
  description, isPartOf: { '@id': `${SITE}/#website` }, publisher: { '@id': `${SITE}/#organization` },
  dateModified: '2026-09-12', about: { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Oral bacterial extracellular vesicle research' }
}, breadcrumb('Science Spine', '/science/')];
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/science/" schema={schema}>
  <article class="science-article">
    <header class="science-hero">
      <p class="section-kicker">Research architecture / Updated September 12, 2026</p>
      <h1>The science<br /><span>behind the connection.</span></h1>
      <p class="science-lede">Not just which bacteria are present.<br />What they release. Where it goes. What it does.</p>
      <p class="section-intro">Fasolati’s science spine asks whether a well-characterized oral bacterial effector measurement can add useful information beyond a periodontal examination, bacterial burden, and conventional risk measures.</p>
      <div class="boundary-note"><strong>Development status.</strong> The OMV assay and individualized systemic-effect interpretations described here remain research proposals. The evidence on this page does not validate a Fasolati oral-rinse test for cardiovascular, neurological, cancer, or other personal disease-risk prediction.</div>
    </header>
    <nav class="science-index" aria-label="Science page contents">
      <a href="#mapping">Mechanism map</a><a href="#human-evidence">Human evidence</a><a href="#measurement">Measurement</a><a href="#experiments">Experiments</a><a href="#life-board">Life Board</a>
    </nav>

    <section class="science-block" id="mapping">
      <div class="section-kicker">The biological map</div>
      <h2>Promising mechanisms.<br />Specific boundaries.</h2>
      <p>Outer membrane vesicles are a subset of bacterial extracellular vesicles, or bEVs. Vesicle identity requires more than a particle’s size, and an enriched preparation can contain non-vesicular material. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10850029/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MISEV2023 guidance</a>.</p>
      <p>This map distinguishes the preparation tested, the reported response, and the claim it cannot support. Open a row to inspect the experimental setting and its limits.</p>
      <div class="mechanism-list">
        {MECHANISMS.map((m, i) => (
          <details class="mechanism">
            <summary><span class="mechanism-num">0{i + 1}</span><span class="mechanism-name"><strong>{m.name}</strong><span>{m.route}</span></span><span class="evidence-tag">{m.setting}</span><span class="expand-sign" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
            <div class="mechanism-body">
              <div><h3>What was observed</h3><p>{m.finding} <a href={m.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{m.source}</a>.</p></div>
              <div><h3>What that does not establish</h3><p>{m.boundary}</p></div>
              <div class="mechanism-use"><h3>Research use</h3><p>{m.use}</p></div>
            </div>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="boundary-note">An experimental effect is not a diagnosis. Evidence about whole bacteria or an isolated molecule is not automatically evidence about an intact vesicle carrying that molecule.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="science-block" id="human-evidence">
      <div class="section-kicker">The human bridge</div>
      <h2>Human evidence exists.<br />Oral-source validation is a separate question.</h2>
      <div class="evidence-pair">
        <div><span class="evidence-tag">Human measurement</span><h3>Circulating bacterial vesicles</h3><p>Tulkens and colleagues characterized bacterial-EV-associated plasma fractions in 49 people, including groups with intestinal-barrier dysfunction. The study provides a measurement precedent, not a human oral-to-blood source trace. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6943244/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tulkens et al.</a></p><p>Ou and colleagues used single-particle LPS/LTA analysis in human cohorts alongside mouse source-discrimination experiments. The paper also discusses residual lipoprotein contamination and unresolved transport questions. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10696524/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ou et al.</a></p></div>
        <div><span class="evidence-tag">Human intervention</span><h3>Periodontal care and systemic markers</h3><p>A Cochrane review found a 0.43 percentage-point reduction in HbA1c at three to four months after periodontal treatment in people with diabetes and periodontitis, with moderate-certainty evidence. This is not evidence that OMVs mediated the effect. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35420698/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Simpson et al., 2022</a>.</p><p>A separate meta-analysis reported lower CRP after periodontal treatment, with substantial heterogeneity. It does not establish that a Fasolati formulation changes inflammation through a vesicle mechanism. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35946825/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Luthra et al., 2023</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <details class="research-detail">
        <summary>A clinical counterweight: the GAIN trial</summary>
        <div><p>The gingipain-inhibitor GAIN trial enrolled 643 participants and missed both co-primary endpoints in the overall cohort. A sponsor-reported salivary-Pg-positive subgroup signal on cognition did not establish benefit on the functional co-primary, and the program encountered liver-safety concerns and a full IND hold. <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1662774/000095017022009040/crtx-20220331.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cortexyme’s 2022 filing</a>.</p><p>This does not disprove all oral-systemic mechanisms. It does show why mechanistic plausibility cannot stand in for successful human treatment, and the subgroup does not validate OMV-based stratification.</p></div>
      </details>
      <p>Within the literature reviewed for this spine, no verified human calibration from a Fasolati oral-rinse vesicle measurement to an individual systemic outcome was located. That is a bounded review finding, not proof that no relevant unpublished or unlocated study exists.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="science-block" id="measurement">
      <div class="section-kicker">The first-principles lens</div>
      <h2>Measure the thing<br />you say you measure.</h2>
      <p>A local sample, a transported exposure, and a host response are different quantities. The proposed architecture keeps four questions distinct until data justify a particular connection.</p>
      <ol class="measurement-steps">
        <li><span>01</span><div><h3>Source ecology</h3><p>Which organisms and local conditions are present? A periodontal examination and bacterial burden provide context, not a direct measure of vesicle activity.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>02</span><div><h3>Effector phenotype</h3><p>What biologically active material is released? Test characterized vesicle-enriched and depleted fractions rather than assuming the effect belongs to vesicles.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>03</span><div><h3>Delivered exposure</h3><p>What reaches a relevant compartment and remains active? Demonstrate the carrier and source instead of relabeling a local rinse signal as systemic load.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>04</span><div><h3>Host response</h3><p>What changes in the recipient? Measure the response independently, then test whether the additional information improves a defined decision.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
      <details class="research-detail"><summary>Why a particle count is not an OMV count</summary><div><p>Nanoparticle tracking analysis detects particles; it does not by itself identify them as bacterial vesicles. Host vesicles, non-vesicular particles, and co-isolated material require appropriate separation and characterization. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10850029/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MISEV2023</a>.</p><p>The proposed assay work must establish identity, recovery, precision, specificity, interference, stability, and a quantifiable range. Neither a bacterial sequence in a mixed fraction nor an inflammatory reporter signal resolves all those questions.</p></div></details>
      <details class="research-detail"><summary>Why fewer particles might not mean less activity</summary><div><p>The development program must compare abundance, cargo, and functional activity separately. A change in sample dilution, analytical recovery, active material per particle, or non-vesicular activity could change the interpretation.</p><p>For oral-rinse intervention studies, residual formulation must be tested for interference with the assay or reporter cells. An apparent reduction in activity is not enough if the rinse itself suppresses the readout.</p></div></details>
      <details class="research-detail"><summary>Why AI cannot fill a missing causal link</summary><div><p>The evidence engine should distinguish a measured result, a validated association, an experimental mechanism, and an untested hypothesis. A fluent explanation cannot turn one category into another.</p><p>SciAgent’s proposed role is to organize evidence and explain uncertainty, not to invent missing measurements or issue autonomous treatment orders. Claims about operational safeguards require verification in the implemented system.</p></div></details>
    </section>

    <section class="science-block" id="experiments">
      <div class="section-kicker">The falsification lens</div>
      <h2>What result would<br />change our minds?</h2>
      <div class="flagship-question"><span>The flagship experiment</span><p>Does characterized vesicle-associated activity add useful information beyond periodontal examination and bacterial count?</p></div>
      <p>These are proposed development gates, not completed Fasolati studies. The sequence is designed to find the weak link before turning it into a customer claim.</p>
      <div class="experiment-list">
        <div><h3>Identity before interpretation</h3><p>Define the analyte, validate the assay in the actual sample matrix, and establish repeatability. Stop personal interpretation if analytical variation overwhelms the signal.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Fraction before mechanism</h3><p>Compare enriched and depleted fractions, then use cargo-specific perturbations and appropriate rescue controls. If the activity is predominantly non-vesicular, follow that result rather than protect the OMV label.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Source before systemic attribution</h3><p>Test oral attribution against gut and other competing sources. Model exposure separately from the recipient’s susceptibility.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Incremental value before a score</h3><p>In a prospective study, test whether the measurement adds information beyond ordinary clinical measures. If it does not, do not add a test merely because it is technologically interesting.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Intervention before efficacy</h3><p>Evaluate the defined finished formulation against an appropriate comparator. Pre/post changes are temporal associations unless the study design supports a causal conclusion.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Utility before routine use</h3><p>Test whether acting on the information helps people more than the alternatives, including ordinary clinical care without the extra test.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="science-block" id="life-board">
      <div class="section-kicker">The translation rule</div>
      <h2>The customer gets clarity.<br />The evidence keeps its limits.</h2>
      <p>The proposed Life Board should answer three questions: what changed, how sure the measurement is, and what next step is justified. It should not average discordant oral and systemic results into a reassuring composite score.</p>
      <div class="evidence-pair">
        <div><h3>On the surface</h3><p>The observation, date, measurement status, and an intelligible next step. Unvalidated measurements remain labeled as research, with no individualized organ-risk interpretation.</p></div>
        <div><h3>One layer deeper</h3><p>The original source, experimental setting, carrier and cargo attribution, dose and route, limitations, and what would be required to support a stronger claim.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p>Established care does not need to “move your score” before it is worthwhile. Product benefit, assay validity, predictive validity, and clinical utility are separate questions that must earn separate answers.</p>
      <p class="boundary-note">This page describes a research program and product direction. It is not a diagnostic service, a treatment protocol, or a substitute for clinical evaluation.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="science-block science-close">
      <div class="section-kicker">Research collaboration</div>
      <h2>Help test the next link.</h2>
      <p>Fasolati welcomes conversations about vesicle characterization, oral-source attribution, functional assays, and prospective clinical validation. The aim is a measurement that deserves to influence a decision.</p>
      <a class="btn bp" href="/partner/">Discuss a research collaboration</a>
      <p class="review-note">Review scope: selected primary vesicle studies, human bEV measurement studies, clinical intervention reviews, and measurement guidance. This is an evidence-informed research architecture, not an exhaustive systematic review.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
